\title{Platt calibrator}
\description{
Binary probability calibration with a univariate logistic sigmoid,
following Platt (2000) and Niculescu-Mizil and Caruana (2005).

By default (\code{label_smoothing = TRUE}), training targets use Platt's Laplace-smoothed
probabilities \(y_+ = (N_+ + 1)/(N_+ + 2)\) and \(y_- = 1/(N_- + 2)\) instead of hard 0/1 labels.
This matches the original proposal and scikit-learn's \code{CalibratedClassifierCV(method = "sigmoid")}.

Set \code{label_smoothing = FALSE} to fit ordinary logistic regression with hard 0/1 labels.
That variant matches tidymodels' \href{https://probably.tidymodels.org/}{probably}
\code{cal_estimate_logistic(..., smooth = FALSE)} and the hard-label "logistic calibration" /
"logistic recalibration" used in clinical prediction (e.g. Steyerberg-style updating on predicted
probabilities).

The predictor can be the raw positive-class probability (\code{input = "probabilities"}, default)
or its logit (\code{input = "logit"}).
Silva Filho et al. (2023) recommend a logit transform when the base model already outputs
probabilities in \eqn{[0, 1]}.
The default keeps the raw probability to match common sklearn / probably practice on
\code{predict_proba} / \verb{.pred_*} columns.

Multiclass Platt calibration is obtained with \code{clb("ovr")}, which wraps this calibrator by default.
}
